# WebSocket Educational Project - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview

This is an educational WebSocket application designed for learning purposes.
Stack: Node.js + ws (backend) | React + Vite (frontend) | Pure WebSocket

## Key Principles

### 1. Code Philosophy

- **Simplicity over abstraction** - No unnecessary complexity
- **Education first** - Code serves as teaching material
- **Comments are mini-tutorials** - ~40% of code should be comments
- **Explicit over implicit** - Show how things work "under the hood"

### 2. Code Style

- Use descriptive variable names
- Add explanatory comments for every concept
- Show ❌ BAD vs ✅ GOOD examples in comments
- Explain WHY, not just WHAT

### 3. What NOT to do

- ❌ Don't add abstractions or complex patterns
- ❌ Don't add production-grade features (auth, rate limiting, etc.)
- ❌ Don't use Socket.io (we want pure WebSocket)
- ❌ Don't remove or reduce comments

### 4. File Structure

```
backend/server.js       - Single file, ~230 lines, heavily commented
frontend/src/hooks/useWebSocket.js - Custom hook with all WS logic
frontend/src/App.jsx    - UI component only, clean separation
```

### 5. When editing code

- Maintain ~40% comment ratio
- Keep code simple and flat
- Use console.log generously (for learning/debugging)
- Every concept should be explained in comments

### 6. Documentation

- Keep documentation up to date with code changes
- Add diagrams when explaining flow
- Use real-world examples
- Include troubleshooting tips

## Common Patterns

### WebSocket in React

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD - useRef for WebSocket
const wsRef = useRef(null);

// ❌ BAD - useState causes unnecessary re-renders
const [ws, setWs] = useState(null);
```

### Cleanup

```javascript
// ✅ ALWAYS cleanup
useEffect(() => {
  const ws = new WebSocket(url);
  return () => ws.close(); // CRITICAL!
}, []);
```

### Broadcasting

```javascript
// ✅ Check readyState before sending
wss.clients.forEach((client) => {
  if (client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
    client.send(data);
  }
});
```

## Files to NOT modify (structure)

- Keep single file backend (server.js)
- Keep single hook frontend (useWebSocket.js)
- Maintain separation between logic (hook) and UI (component)

## When asked to add features

1. First check if it aligns with educational goals
2. Keep it simple
3. Add comprehensive comments
4. Update documentation if needed
5. Show both working example and potential pitfalls

Remember: This is NOT production code. It's a learning tool! 🎓
